---
/**
 * `.atl` FOUNDATION — the shared atlas design system for the three LEARNING
 * surfaces (`/domain/*`, `/topic/*`, `/study/*`).
 *
 * WHY THIS IS A COMPONENT AND `.ins` IS NOT.
 * CONTRACT.md §12 keeps the `.ins` foundation duplicated verbatim between
 * index.astro and catalog.astro on the grounds that "two pages are not a
 * library". atlas has THREE consumers and ~330 lines, so copying it would mean
 * ~990 lines of custom properties that must stay byte-identical across three
 * files — the exact drift this component removes. Nothing is rendered here but
 * a `<style is:global>` block, so the component costs one import per page.
 *
 * THE SIX AUTHORING RULES OF §12 APPLY UNCHANGED, and `.atl` continues their
 * numbering discipline:
 *  1. Every selector contains `.atl`. Write `.atl .atl-cell { … }`, never
 *     `.atl-cell { … }` — (0,2,0) beats any single Tailwind utility (0,1,0), so
 *     the outcome never depends on bundler emit order, and a lingering
 *     stylesheet cannot restyle another page after a ClientRouter navigation.
 *  2. Never hardcode an accent hex. The channel reaches every primitive as ONE
 *     custom property, `--atl-ch`, whose default is the page's existing
 *     `--topic-accent` and whose per-page value comes from the markup (see
 *     `@lib/atlas`), so the light theme's deepened accents apply automatically.
 *  3. Consuming a shared token is fine; mutating one is not. Every declaration
 *     below sits on `.atl` or `[data-theme="light"] .atl` — never `:root`,
 *     never `[data-theme="light"]` alone.
 *  4. Mono is a LABEL face: no clause, no terminal full stop, <=3 words a unit
 *     (`LEG 02`, `5 / 13`, `NOT VISITED`).
 *  5. A channel colour is a hairline mark, never a field. See the plane rules
 *     next to `--atl-ch` below for the measured contrast limits.
 *  6. One animated object per page, one named timeline, never two `view()`s on
 *     differently-sized subjects. `.atl-in` is the only primitive here that
 *     moves, its range length is the subject's own height, and it must never go
 *     on a `<section>` or a 1-2px hairline.
 *
 * `.atl` ITSELF DECLARES ONLY CUSTOM PROPERTIES — no background, colour, face,
 * size or leading. BaseLayout already owns the canvas and the Inter face, and
 * keeping `.atl` properties-only is what lets a page add the class without any
 * visual change at all. The single deliberate exception is `--prose-body`
 * (GRAFT 1), documented where it is declared.
 */
---

<style is:global>
  .atl {
    /* ── faces ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Mono only. The prose/UI face stays Inter, inherited from BaseLayout:
       the atlas direction takes reading-room's SIZE, never its serif. ─── */
    --atl-mono: "JetBrains Mono Variable", "JetBrains Mono", ui-monospace,
                SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Monaco, Consolas, monospace;

    /* ── type ladder ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       The nine `--ins-t-*` steps, same clamp() coefficients, same 375 -> 1280px
       floor/cap, so a learning page and a marketing page typeset from one
       ladder. Two PROSE-ONLY steps are added: a study H2 must sit BELOW a
       marketing H2 (40px would out-shout the page title) and clearly above its
       own H3, and there was no step in the `--ins-*` set between 26 and 28px
       at the top or 20 and 22px below it. ───────────────────────────────── */
    --atl-t-micro: clamp(0.75rem,   0.7241rem + 0.1105vw, 0.8125rem); /* 12 → 13 */
    --atl-t-label: clamp(0.8125rem, 0.7866rem + 0.1105vw, 0.875rem);  /* 13 → 14 */
    --atl-t-body:  clamp(1rem,      0.9482rem + 0.221vw,  1.125rem);  /* 16 → 18 */
    --atl-t-lead:  clamp(1.125rem,  1.0732rem + 0.221vw,  1.25rem);   /* 18 → 20 */
    --atl-t-ph3:   clamp(1.1875rem, 1.1357rem + 0.221vw,  1.3125rem); /* 19 → 21 — prose H3 */
    --atl-t-h3:    clamp(1.375rem,  1.2714rem + 0.442vw,  1.625rem);  /* 22 → 26 */
    --atl-t-ph2:   clamp(1.5rem,    1.3964rem + 0.442vw,  1.75rem);   /* 24 → 28 — prose H2 */
    --atl-t-h4:    clamp(1.5rem,    1.3964rem + 0.442vw,  1.75rem);   /* 24 → 28 */
    --atl-t-fig:   clamp(1.625rem,  1.2624rem + 1.547vw,  2.5rem);    /* 26 → 40 */
    --atl-t-h2:    clamp(1.75rem,   1.4392rem + 1.326vw,  2.5rem);    /* 28 → 40 */
    --atl-t-h1:    clamp(2.25rem,   1.4213rem + 3.536vw,  4.25rem);   /* 36 → 68 */

    /* ── GRAFT 1: the prose body step, raised. MEASURED, not reasoned ────
       Headless Chromium against this worktree's own /study page, 1440px
       viewport: the article column is 984px, Inter's average advance in the
       prose is 0.4818em (10.35px at the desktop body) — and at 1440px THE
       COLUMN BINDS, not the measure. 17.6px therefore paints 116 characters per
       line. 19.8px paints 95 in the IDENTICAL 984px column (re-measured, not
       extrapolated).

       The line gets shorter in CHARACTERS by getting bigger in TYPE, and
       `--measure-wide` stays 80ch with leading untouched at 1.63 / 1.68 —
       which, at the larger size, means the leading rises in absolute terms
       (29.6px -> 33.3px) exactly as the graft requires.

       FOLLOW-UP, and the reason the measure is no longer 104ch: the bigger face
       alone did not hold on a wide monitor. The grid binds up to ~2030px, so the
       line grew back to 118 characters at 1920px. The study page therefore CAPS
       `--measure` at 79ch (= 987px = ~95 characters) at >=1024px, which changes
       nothing at or below 1440px because the grid still binds there. See the
       study page's own `(2)` comment and CONTRACT.md's GRAFT 1 section.

       Delivered through `--prose-body`, the fallback hook global.css's `.prose`
       rule already reads (`font-size: var(--prose-body, clamp(…))`), so no
       font-size rule is added and the OpenQuestions panel — which reads the
       same token — stays in step with the article. One continuous clamp, so
       there is no size jump at the 1024px breakpoint. ─────────────────────── */
    --atl-prose-body: clamp(1.075rem, 1.0077rem + 0.2873vw, 1.2375rem); /* 17.2 → 19.8 */
    --prose-body: var(--atl-prose-body);

    /* ── ink: three values, strictly monotonic in both themes ───────────
       `--atl-ink-3` is new relative to `--ins-*`: atlas has a third tier of
       type (legends, spine numerals, "not visited") that must still clear AA,
       and reusing `--ins-ink-2` for it flattened the hierarchy. Measured on the
       darkest ground each is allowed to sit on — see the plane note below. */
    --atl-ink:   #ffffff;   /* 21:1 on plane-0, 17.40:1 on plane-2 */
    --atl-ink-2: #999999;   /* 7.37:1 on plane-0, 6.11:1 on plane-2 */
    --atl-ink-3: #8a8a8a;   /* 6.08:1 on plane-0, 5.04:1 on plane-2 */

    /* ── planes: elevation as LUMINANCE ─────────────────────────────────
       Identical to `--ins-plane-*`: --shadow-1/2/3 are all oklch(0 0 0 / …) on
       a #000 canvas and render zero pixels in dark. L* ≈ 0 / 4.7 / 9.3 / 14.2. */
    --atl-plane-0: #000000;
    --atl-plane-1: #101010;
    --atl-plane-2: #1a1a1a;
    --atl-plane-3: #242424;
    --atl-rule:        #262626;   /* same-plane divider / row rule — DECORATIVE */
    --atl-rule-strong: #3a3a3a;   /* plane boundary / rail / axis — DECORATIVE */

    /* An unvisited node and an unvisited meter cell are STATE-BEARING
       GRAPHICS: they are the "not covered" half of a two-state mark, so they
       owe 3:1, which `--atl-rule-strong` (a rail) does not. The mock proposed
       #5a5a5a; measured, that is 2.76:1 on plane-1 and 2.70:1 on plane-2, where
       the spine's hover row and the route drawer put these marks. #6e6e6e
       clears 3:1 on ALL FOUR planes (4.12 / 3.74 / 3.42 / 3.05). */
    --atl-node-ring:   #6e6e6e;

    /* ── brand ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       The `--ins-brand` values under an `--atl-*` name, because `--ins-brand`
       resolves to NOTHING outside a `.ins` wrapper — and the Design track's
       channel token in @lib/channels is literally `var(--ins-brand)`. See
       `@lib/atlas`, which is the one place that translation happens. */
    --atl-brand:      #FF6B00;  /* 7.36:1 on plane-0 */
    --atl-brand-ink:  #FF8A2B;  /* 7.09:1 on plane-3 — headline emphasis */
    --atl-on-brand:   #14100c;  /* 6.57:1 on the orange fill */
    --atl-focus:      #FF8A2B;

    /* ── the channel: ONE property, set from the markup ──────────────────
       Default is the page's existing `--topic-accent`, so a page that has not
       been given a channel yet still resolves to something correct rather than
       to nothing. MEASURED LIMITS over all seven tracks, and rule 5 depends on
       them:
         · as a GRAPHIC (tick, node, cell, 1px segment, 2px rule): >=3:1 on
           planes 0-2 in both themes — fine everywhere atlas draws.
         · as TEXT: PLANE-0 ONLY. The mock's notes guessed dark violet #8B5CF6
           (the Craft & Data channel) at 4.51:1 on plane-1; computed, it is
           4.49:1 — it fails AA, so plane-1 is out for channel text, not merely
           tight. On plane-2 it is 4.11:1 and light amber #9a6700 is 4.30:1.
           Exactly two primitives below take the channel as text, both on the
           page ground; `atlas-css.test.ts` asserts that list by name so a third
           cannot appear quietly. Everywhere else the channel is a mark and the
           words beside it are `--atl-ink`. */
    --atl-ch: var(--topic-accent, var(--color-primary));

    /* The permanent cost of the locator bar, as one number so the scroll-margin
       that has to clear it cannot drift away from the bar's own height. */
    --atl-locator-h: 46px;
  }

  [data-theme="light"] .atl {
    --atl-ink:   #1f2328;   /* 15.80:1 on plane-0, 13.94:1 on plane-2 */
    --atl-ink-2: #57606a;   /* 6.49:1 on plane-0, 5.64:1 on plane-2 */
    --atl-ink-3: #656d76;   /* 5.30:1 on plane-0, 4.63:1 on plane-2 */

    /* The ladder INVERTS: in light theme "raised" means paler. */
    --atl-plane-0: #ffffff;
    --atl-plane-1: #f6f8fa;
    --atl-plane-2: #eef1f4;
    --atl-plane-3: #e7ebef;
    --atl-rule:        #d0d7de;
    --atl-rule-strong: #afb8c1;

    /* #8c959f (the mock's value) is 2.85:1 on plane-1 and 2.89:1 on plane-2.
       #7d8590 clears 3:1 on all four light planes (3.73 / 3.50 / 3.29 / 3.11). */
    --atl-node-ring:   #7d8590;

    --atl-brand:      #B54708;  /* 5.43:1 as text on plane-0 AND for #fff on the fill */
    --atl-brand-ink:  #B54708;
    --atl-on-brand:   #ffffff;
    --atl-focus:      #B54708;
  }

  /* ── type primitives ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .atl .atl-h1 {
    font-size: var(--atl-t-h1); font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.04;
    letter-spacing: -0.022em; color: var(--atl-ink);
    max-width: 20ch; margin: 0; text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .atl .atl-h2 {
    font-size: var(--atl-t-h4); font-weight: 650; line-height: 1.14;
    letter-spacing: -0.016em; color: var(--atl-ink); margin: 0; max-width: 26ch;
  }
  .atl .atl-h3 {
    font-size: var(--atl-t-ph3); font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.25;
    letter-spacing: -0.012em; color: var(--atl-ink); margin: 0;
  }
  .atl .atl-lead {
    font-size: var(--atl-t-lead); font-weight: 400; line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--atl-ink-2); max-width: 58ch; margin: 0;
  }
  .atl .atl-body {
    font-size: var(--atl-t-body); font-weight: 400; line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--atl-ink-2); max-width: 62ch; margin: 0;
  }
  /* ONE all-caps label recipe, mirroring `.ins-label`. */
  .atl .atl-label {
    font-family: var(--atl-mono); font-size: var(--atl-t-label); font-weight: 500;
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.10em; line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--atl-ink-2); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; margin: 0;
  }
  .atl .atl-micro {
    font-family: var(--atl-mono); font-size: var(--atl-t-micro); font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.4; color: var(--atl-ink-2);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums slashed-zero; margin: 0;
  }
  .atl .atl-fig {
    font-family: var(--atl-mono); font-size: var(--atl-t-fig); font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1; letter-spacing: 0; color: var(--atl-ink);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums slashed-zero;
  }
  .atl .atl-fig-sm {
    font-family: var(--atl-mono); font-size: var(--atl-t-h3); font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.15; color: var(--atl-ink);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums slashed-zero;
  }
  .atl .atl-hl { color: var(--atl-brand-ink); font-weight: inherit; }

  /* ── THE SPINE: route line + waypoints ────────────────────────────────
     The breadcrumb drawn as a transit line. Segments BEHIND you take the
     channel, segments ahead stay hairline. Every waypoint is a 44px-tall
     target; below 640px the `--far` waypoints drop, and their destinations
     are still one tap away through the ones that remain. Radius is 1px: a map
     is drawn with a pen, not a rounded-rect tool. ─────────────────────── */
  .atl .atl-route { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--atl-rule); }
  .atl .atl-route__list {
    display: flex; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap;
    list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; min-height: 44px;
  }
  .atl .atl-wp { display: flex; align-items: center; }
  .atl .atl-wp__seg {
    flex: 0 0 1rem; width: 1rem; height: 1px; background: var(--atl-rule-strong);
  }
  .atl .atl-wp--passed .atl-wp__seg { background: var(--atl-ch); }
  .atl .atl-wp__node {
    flex: 0 0 7px; width: 7px; height: 7px; border-radius: 1px;
    border: 1px solid var(--atl-node-ring);
    background: var(--atl-plane-0); margin-right: 0.5rem;
  }
  .atl .atl-wp--passed .atl-wp__node {
    background: var(--atl-ch); border-color: var(--atl-ch);
  }
  .atl .atl-wp--here .atl-wp__node {
    flex-basis: 11px; width: 11px; height: 11px;
    background: var(--atl-ch); border-color: var(--atl-ch);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px color-mix(in oklab, var(--atl-ch) 24%, transparent);
  }
  .atl .atl-wp a,
  .atl .atl-wp > span:last-child {
    display: flex; align-items: center; min-height: 44px; padding-right: 0.25rem;
    font-family: var(--atl-mono); font-size: var(--atl-t-micro); font-weight: 500;
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--atl-ink-2); text-decoration: none; white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .atl .atl-wp a:hover { color: var(--atl-ink); text-decoration: underline; }
  .atl .atl-wp--here > span:last-child { color: var(--atl-ink); }
  @media (max-width: 639px) {
    .atl .atl-wp--far { display: none; }
    .atl .atl-wp--mhead .atl-wp__seg { display: none; }
  }

  /* ── CELLS: progress as ground covered, never a percentage ────────────
     One cell per place. `flex: 1 1 0` with a 5px floor is what makes the meter
     survive a domain with far more subtopics than Docker's 16 — past the point
     where the floor wins, the meter scrolls its own overflow rather than
     pushing the page sideways. A cell is a GRAPHIC, so `--atl-node-ring` (3:1)
     is the unfilled state; nothing here is conveyed by colour alone, because
     every meter ships with `.atl-legend`. ────────────────────────────── */
  .atl .atl-meter {
    display: flex; align-items: flex-end; gap: 3px; height: 13px;
    overflow-x: auto; overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
  }
  .atl .atl-cell {
    flex: 1 1 0; min-width: 5px; height: 6px; border-radius: 1px;
    background: var(--atl-node-ring);
  }
  .atl .atl-cell.is-read { background: var(--atl-ch); }
  .atl .atl-cell.is-here { background: var(--atl-ch); height: 13px; }

  /* A map without a key is decoration. */
  .atl .atl-legend {
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.25rem 1.1rem;
    list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0;
  }
  .atl .atl-legend li {
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.45rem;
    font-family: var(--atl-mono); font-size: var(--atl-t-micro);
    color: var(--atl-ink-3); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.07em;
  }
  .atl .atl-key {
    width: 9px; height: 9px; border-radius: 1px; flex: none;
    border: 1px solid var(--atl-node-ring); background: var(--atl-plane-0);
  }
  .atl .atl-key--read { background: var(--atl-ch); border-color: var(--atl-ch); }
  .atl .atl-key--here {
    background: var(--atl-ch); border-color: var(--atl-ch);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 2px color-mix(in oklab, var(--atl-ch) 30%, transparent);
  }

  /* ── THE LOCATOR: a 46px shell, and that is the whole permanent cost ──
     The bar is `position: sticky`, so it costs 46px of viewport once and never
     reflows the prose. Its disclosure drawer OVERLAYS (`position: absolute` off
     `.atl-locator__in`), which is the property that keeps the reading measure
     at zero cost. The shell is the primitive; each surface fills it. ──── */
  .atl .atl-locator {
    position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 40;
    background: var(--atl-plane-1);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--atl-rule);
  }
  .atl .atl-locator__in {
    position: relative;
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.75rem;
    min-height: var(--atl-locator-h);
  }
  /* The whole readout is the <summary>, so the tap target is the bar. */
  .atl .atl-locator__sum {
    display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.6rem;
    min-height: var(--atl-locator-h); cursor: pointer; list-style: none;
  }
  .atl .atl-locator__sum::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  /* The bar's ground is plane-1, where the Craft & Data channel measures 4.49:1
     as text — so the folio is INK and the channel's presence in the bar is the
     meter's `is-here` cell, a graphic. */
  .atl .atl-locator__folio {
    flex: none;
    font-family: var(--atl-mono); font-size: var(--atl-t-micro); font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em; color: var(--atl-ink);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums slashed-zero;
  }
  .atl .atl-locator__sec {
    flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0;
    overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis; white-space: nowrap;
    font-size: var(--atl-t-micro); font-weight: 500; color: var(--atl-ink);
  }
  .atl .atl-locator__count {
    flex: none;
    font-family: var(--atl-mono); font-size: var(--atl-t-micro);
    color: var(--atl-ink-3); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums slashed-zero;
  }
  .atl .atl-locator__meter { flex: 0 0 108px; width: 108px; gap: 2px; }
  @media (max-width: 767px) { .atl .atl-locator__meter { display: none; } }
  /* The drawer overlays; it never pushes the prose down. */
  .atl .atl-drawer {
    position: absolute; left: 0; right: 0; top: 100%; z-index: 41;
    max-height: min(60vh, 26rem); overflow-y: auto;
    background: var(--atl-plane-2);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--atl-rule-strong);
    box-shadow: 0 12px 28px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.35);
    padding: 0.5rem 0 0.75rem;
  }
  [data-theme="light"] .atl .atl-drawer { box-shadow: 0 12px 28px rgb(31 35 40 / 0.14); }
  /* A glyph caret, not a rotated box: the marketing guard's zero-rotation
     thesis is the house style, and a `transform: rotate()` on a 9px box is
     also the one thing that renders soft on a fractional position. */
  .atl .atl-caret {
    flex: none; width: 0.75rem; text-align: center;
    color: var(--atl-ink-3); font-size: 0.7rem; line-height: 1;
  }
  .atl .atl-caret::before { content: "\25BC"; }
  .atl [open] > .atl-locator__sum .atl-caret::before { content: "\25B2"; }

  /* ── the printed-key primitive: DECLARED, CURRENTLY UNUSED ────────────
     `.atl-kbd` is a printed keycap; `.atl-keys` is the row it sits in. It has
     no consumer today: the single-character `R`/`P`/`D` route accelerators that
     used to print these chips were WITHDRAWN for violating WCAG 2.1.4
     Character Key Shortcuts — see CONTRACT.md §13.1 for the full reasoning.
     The primitive stays declared because a keycap is a general typographic
     need (documenting a browser or OS chord, for instance). Anything that
     revives it must satisfy 2.1.4 FIRST: an off switch, a remap, or activation
     only while a specific component holds focus. ─────────────────────── */
  .atl .atl-keys {
    display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: 0.4rem 0.9rem;
    margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none;
  }
  .atl .atl-kbd {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    min-width: 1.35rem; height: 1.35rem; padding: 0 0.3rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--atl-rule-strong); border-radius: 2px;
    background: var(--atl-plane-1); color: var(--atl-ink-2);
    font-family: var(--atl-mono); font-size: var(--atl-t-micro); font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  /* ── section head: mono folio + a hairline to the container edge ───────
     NOTHING is ever animated on this pseudo-element: a 1px-tall ::after is a
     1px-tall timeline subject, so an `entry` range on it resolves to about one
     pixel of scroll and snaps instead of drawing (§12 rule 6). */
  .atl .atl-sechead {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: auto 1fr; align-items: center;
    gap: 0.875rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .atl .atl-sechead::after { content: ""; height: 1px; background: var(--atl-rule); }

  /* ── the route onward: every page ends in a NAMED destination ────────── */
  .atl .atl-onward {
    display: block; text-decoration: none; color: inherit;
    border-top: 2px solid var(--atl-ch);
    background: var(--atl-plane-1); padding: 1.25rem 1.25rem 1.4rem;
  }
  .atl .atl-onward:hover { background: var(--atl-plane-2); }

  /* ── controls ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     44px floors everywhere, 1-2px radius, hairline borders instead of boxes. */
  .atl .atl-cta {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.5rem; min-height: 44px; padding: 0 1.1rem;
    background: var(--atl-brand); color: var(--atl-on-brand);
    font-weight: 650; font-size: var(--atl-t-label); text-decoration: none;
    border: 1px solid var(--atl-brand); border-radius: 2px;
  }
  .atl .atl-cta:hover {
    background: var(--atl-brand-ink); border-color: var(--atl-brand-ink);
  }
  /* A progressive-enhancement route ships with the `hidden` ATTRIBUTE and is
     revealed only once a store has been read. `.atl .atl-cta` is (0,2,0) and
     Tailwind's `[hidden] { display: none }` reset is (0,1,0), so without this the
     CTA would stay on screen while being hidden from the a11y tree — the worst
     of both. Inert for every visible element, by construction. */
  .atl .atl-cta[hidden] { display: none; }
  .atl .atl-cta--ghost {
